1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,408
This is step three of the clustering course: spectral bisection.

2
00:00:05,408 --> 00:00:11,308
We start with a graph, give every node a score, and turn that score into a cut.

3
00:00:11,308 --> 00:00:15,508
Three steps, one tiny graph, no hand-waving.

4
00:00:16,358 --> 00:00:23,410
Here is the problem. Five nodes, six weighted edges, and we want two clusters.

5
00:00:23,410 --> 00:00:30,054
Two rules. Keep the cut small, and keep the two sides similar in size.

6
00:00:30,054 --> 00:00:34,730
A small cut alone is trivial: just isolate one node.

7
00:00:34,730 --> 00:00:37,922
That is why the balance rule exists.

8
00:00:38,772 --> 00:00:47,528
Look at the weights. Inside A, B and C the edges are five, four and one, so they are tightly bound.

9
00:00:47,528 --> 00:00:51,052
D and E are joined by five as well.

10
00:00:51,052 --> 00:00:56,232
Between the two groups there is only a weak bridge: two, and one.

11
00:00:56,232 --> 00:01:05,232
So the answer is almost visible. A, B, C on one side, D and E on the other, cut equals three.

12
00:01:06,082 --> 00:01:09,558
Step one: build the Laplacian.

13
00:01:09,558 --> 00:01:17,282
The diagonal holds each node's weighted degree. Every edge puts minus its weight off the diagonal.

14
00:01:17,282 --> 00:01:21,382
Every row sums to zero, and that is your check.

15
00:01:21,382 --> 00:01:26,758
It is the same graph, rewritten so that linear algebra can work with it.

16
00:01:27,608 --> 00:01:37,852
Why this matrix? Because x transpose L x equals the sum over edges of weight times the squared difference of the endpoint values.

17
00:01:37,852 --> 00:01:44,520
In words: if two strongly connected nodes get very different values, the cost is high.

18
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:49,244
The matrix measures disagreement, weighted by importance.

19
00:01:49,244 --> 00:01:54,812
So the eigenvector that minimises this cost is the one that keeps clusters together.

20
00:01:55,662 --> 00:02:00,050
Step two: take eigenvectors of the Laplacian.

21
00:02:00,050 --> 00:02:07,006
The smallest one is flat. Every node gets the same value, so it says nothing.

22
00:02:07,006 --> 00:02:13,170
The second smallest is the Fiedler vector, and it is the smoothest non-flat assignment.

23
00:02:13,170 --> 00:02:21,042
Here it gives E and D negative, and C, B, A positive. The sign is already a suggestion.

24
00:02:21,892 --> 00:02:25,056
Step three begins by sorting.

25
00:02:25,056 --> 00:02:32,612
E, then D, then C, then B, then A. The graph collapses to one line.

26
00:02:32,612 --> 00:02:37,844
Any cut between two neighbours is a candidate, so there are four candidates.

27
00:02:38,694 --> 00:02:42,122
So try every balanced prefix.

28
00:02:42,122 --> 00:02:45,622
k equals one: cut six.

29
00:02:45,622 --> 00:02:48,882
k equals two: cut three.

30
00:02:48,882 --> 00:02:52,310
k equals three: cut five.

31
00:02:52,310 --> 00:02:55,738
k equals four: cut six.

32
00:02:55,738 --> 00:03:00,922
Keep the smallest inside the window, and the choice is k equals two.

33
00:03:01,772 --> 00:03:05,680
Notice that the middle of the order is not the answer.

34
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:09,828
k equals three scores five, not three.

35
00:03:09,828 --> 00:03:16,160
The middle is a guess based on counting nodes, and counting knows nothing about edge weights.

36
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:20,624
Scores propose. The sweep decides.

37
00:03:21,474 --> 00:03:26,774
The result: A, B, C against D and E, cut three.

38
00:03:26,774 --> 00:03:32,770
C D is two, C E is one, so the two heavy edges never get cut.

39
00:03:32,770 --> 00:03:39,730
This is the same answer that greedy and label propagation find, but it appears without merging anything.

40
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:42,808
Three traps.

41
00:03:42,808 --> 00:03:47,772
The eigenvector can flip sign; the partition is unchanged.

42
00:03:47,772 --> 00:03:53,624
A disconnected graph makes lambda two zero, so check components first.

43
00:03:53,624 --> 00:03:58,588
And ties in the scores need a stable sort, or the cut can wobble.

44
00:03:58,588 --> 00:04:07,276
Now open the lab, run the sweep yourself, and compare with this table. Next module: Kernighan Lin refinement.

